# Component Authoring Rules

## File structure — strict

Every component lives in its own folder under `src/components/`:

```
src/components/Button/
├── Button.tsx           # The component (wraps MUI)
├── Button.types.ts      # TypeScript interfaces
├── Button.examples.tsx  # Code examples for docs + MCP
└── index.ts             # Re-exports for clean imports
```

The folder name matches the component name (PascalCase). The `index.ts` re-exports everything:

```tsx
// src/components/Button/index.ts
export { Button } from './Button';
export type { ButtonProps } from './Button.types';
```

## TypeScript rules

- **Always export the props interface.** Consumers need to extend it.
- **Use `Omit` when extending MUI props** to remove props we're remapping:
  ```tsx
  interface ButtonProps extends Omit<MuiButtonProps, 'variant' | 'size' | 'color'> {
    variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline' | 'ghost' | 'destructive';
    size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg';
  }
  ```
- **Use string literal unions** for variant props, not enums.
- **Use `React.forwardRef`** for components that wrap interactive MUI elements (Button, TextField, etc.) so refs work correctly.

## Component implementation rules

### 1. Wrap, never reimplement

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong — reimplementing from scratch
export const Button = (props) => (
  <button style={{ padding: '8px 16px', background: '#3B82F6' }}>
    {props.children}
  </button>
);

// ✅ Right — wrap MUI
import { Button as MuiButton } from '@mui/material';
export const Button = ({ variant = 'primary', ...props }: ButtonProps) => (
  <MuiButton {...variantMap[variant]} {...props} />
);
```

### 2. Sensible defaults

Every variant/size prop must have a default:

```tsx
export const Button = ({
  variant = 'primary',   // ✅ has default
  size = 'md',            // ✅ has default
  ...props
}: ButtonProps) => { /* ... */ };
```

### 3. Always set `displayName`

```tsx
Button.displayName = 'Button';
```

This makes React DevTools and error messages readable.

### 4. JSDoc the component

```tsx
/**
 * Primary action component. Use for all clickable actions.
 *
 * @example
 * <Button variant="primary" size="md">Submit</Button>
 *
 * @example
 * <Button variant="destructive">Delete</Button>
 */
export const Button = (...) => { /* ... */ };
```

The MCP generator reads JSDoc and includes it in `get_component` responses.

## Examples file — required

Every component must have a `<ComponentName>.examples.tsx` file:

```tsx
// Button.examples.tsx
export const examples = [
  {
    title: 'Primary button',
    code: '<Button variant="primary" size="md">Submit</Button>',
  },
  {
    title: 'Destructive action',
    code: '<Button variant="destructive">Delete account</Button>',
  },
  {
    title: 'Loading state',
    code: '<Button loading>Processing...</Button>',
  },
];

export const dos = [
  'Use `primary` for the main CTA on a page (one per section)',
  'Use `destructive` for irreversible actions',
];

export const donts = [
  'Don\'t use multiple `primary` buttons in the same section',
  'Don\'t use `destructive` for cancel actions',
];
```

The MCP generator reads these and serves them via `get_component`. They also appear on the docs site.

## Public exports

After creating a component, add it to `src/index.ts`:

```tsx
// src/index.ts
export { Button } from './components/Button';
export type { ButtonProps } from './components/Button';

export { TextField } from './components/TextField';
export type { TextFieldProps } from './components/TextField';

// ... etc
```

The MCP server only knows about components exported from `src/index.ts`.

## Naming conventions

- **Components:** PascalCase (`Button`, `TextField`, `MetricCard`)
- **Files:** Match component name (`Button.tsx`, not `button.tsx`)
- **Props interfaces:** `<ComponentName>Props` (`ButtonProps`, `TextFieldProps`)
- **Variant names:** lowercase, semantic (`primary`, `destructive`, not `blue`, `red`)
- **Size names:** abbreviated (`sm`, `md`, `lg`) — never `small`/`medium`/`large` in our API (MUI uses long form internally; we map)
- **Icons:** never `@phosphor-icons/react` directly outside `src/icons`, and always use the `Icon`-suffixed export — `CaretDownIcon`, not `CaretDown`. Phosphor 2.1 deprecated every bare name; both still resolve, so the compiler will not stop you, only the editor's strikethrough. phosphoricons.com lists names unsuffixed: add `Icon` to what you find there.

  Which module you import from depends on whether the code ships in the library bundle:

  - **A component in `src/components/**`** imports from `@/src/icons/glyphs`, and adds the glyph to that file's export list if it is not there yet. `glyphs.ts` names every icon explicitly for a reason that is not style: Phosphor is `external` in `tsup.config.ts`, so a bundled `export *` compiles to a runtime namespace object and every consumer retains all 1,512 icons — about 5 MB in an app that imported one button. `src/icons/glyphs.ts` documents the mechanism in full.
  - **Docs and app code (`app/**`)** keeps `@/src/icons`, which is the whole set. It stands in for the `@neofloai/atoms/icons` subpath a consumer writes, so demo code reads the way real code does, and Next resolves the star from source and shakes it. The docs site is not a published bundle.

  `npm run check:icons` fails the build if the namespace object comes back. Do not silence it — the leak is invisible in the published output, which is why the check exists.

## Client vs Server components

Atoms components are **always client components**. They use MUI which requires React context:

```tsx
'use client';

import { Button as MuiButton } from '@mui/material';
// ...
```

If you forget `'use client'`, MUI will throw a "Context not found" error at runtime.
